<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yohl Ikʼnal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 583–<a href="/years/604">604</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Lady Kan Ik, Lady Kʼanal Ikʼnal, Ix Yohl Ikʼnal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yohl Ikʼnal was queen regnant of the Maya <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palenque">Palenque</a>, ancient Lakamha, from her accession on 23 December 583 CE until her death on 7 November 604. She is the first definitively identified woman in recorded Maya history to have held full royal authority and the complete royal title of kʼuhul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajaw">ajaw</a>, divine lord or lady. In 599 Palenque was sacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calakmul">Calakmul</a>, probably on the orders of its ruler Scroll Serpent, and likely paid it tribute, but she went on ruling and was succeeded by Ajen Yohl Mat, apparently her son. Her grandson or great-grandson Kʼinich Janaab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinich-janaab-pakal">Pakal</a> I stressed his descent from her, and she is shown twice on his sarcophagus.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yohl Ikʼnal belonged to the royal Bʼaakal dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palenque">Palenque</a> and descended from its founder, Kʼukʼ Bahlam I. Her parentage is unclear because the hieroglyphic records are ambiguous: most scholars hold that she was the daughter either of Kan Bahlam I, her immediate predecessor, or of Kʼan Joy Chitam I, who ruled earlier in the sixth century. Her tie to Janahb Pakal is also uncertain; inscriptions describe a parent-child bond, and scholars are divided over whether he was her consort or her son. Through Lady <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sak-k-uk">Sak Kʼukʼ</a>, the second woman to rule Palenque, she was an ancestor of Kʼinich Janaab Pakal I.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Classic Maya kingship passed almost always through the male line, and the circumstances of her accession have not survived. Scholars have suggested that no son of the previous ruler was old enough to reign, that a disputed succession or outside threat called for the strongest claimant, or that she first served as regent for a young male who died. The first sixteen years of her reign appear to have been stable, though the record for them is thin.</p><p>On 23 April 599 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palenque">Palenque</a> suffered an och kʼahkʼ, fire-entering, the standard Maya expression for the sacking of a city. Some inscriptions name a lord of Santa Elena as the direct attacker, suggesting that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calakmul">Calakmul</a> acted through a dependent polity while claiming the victory. The city and its dynasty survived, and there are indications that either Yohl Ikʼnal in her last years or her successor Ajen Yohl Mat threw off Calakmul's dominance before 611.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>She died on 7 November 604, recorded in the Long Count as 9.8.11.6.12 2 Eb 20 Keh, after twenty-one years on the throne; the cause of her death is not recorded. The archaeologist Merle Greene Robertson has suggested that an intact vaulted tomb beneath Temple 20, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-the-inscriptions">Temple of the Inscriptions</a>, may be hers, pointing to its date, its rich burial goods and the small size of the skeleton, though other scholars identify it differently. Besides her two images on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinich-janaab-pakal">Pakal</a>'s sarcophagus, she was sculpted among the ancestors on the walls of his tomb, and she is named on the Palace Tablet, on three panels of the Temple of the Inscriptions and at Temple 17.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yohl Ikʼnal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Yohl Ikʼnal</b>, also known as Lady Kan Ik and Lady Kʼanal Ikʼnal, acceded to the throne of the Maya city-state of Palenque as queen regnant.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-23">23 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
